metrics/agent-dashboard.html
sessions/S-*.json
runtime-continuity/guard/

# Token usage ledger — raw runtime evidence is high-frequency local-only
# state; only the dated tar.gz archives under archives/ are committed.
token-attempts/receipts/
token-attempts/ledger-index.json
token-attempts/recovery-events.jsonl

# Graphify plugin runtime state. The shared implementation under
# plugins/graphify/lib/ IS framework-owned and tracked; the marker files
# beside it are local runtime state and must never enter the source tree.
plugins/graphify/.hook-installed
plugins/graphify/.hook-unwired.json

# Graphify edge-density guard runtime state (issue #154). The density
# baseline and the rebuild-cooldown marker are local runtime state: the
# baseline describes THIS checkout's graph and must not be shared between
# machines, and the cooldown marker is meaningless elsewhere.
plugins/graphify/.density-baseline.json
plugins/graphify/.density-rebuild-state.json

# The monotonic counters of the density guard. Every number that was
# handed out is a file of its own, so the counters can never go backwards;
# they are local runtime state and must not be shared between machines.
plugins/graphify/.density-tickets/

# The registry of the lock holders that LOST the lock. It is keyed by the
# lock token, and the readers drop everything a displaced holder published.
plugins/graphify/.density-displaced/

# The publication gate of the density guard. Every participant of the
# rebuild protocol holds a file of its own while it is inside the protocol,
# and the files are removed when the participant is done. They are local
# runtime state and must not be shared between machines.
plugins/graphify/.density-gates/

# The staging area of the guarded graph update. The writer of the graph
# produces its output in a directory of its own, and the framework
# publishes it into `graphify-out/` when the update was allowed to publish.
# A discarded staging area is local runtime state.
plugins/graphify/.density-staging/

# The counter of an older version of the guard. It is superseded by the
# ticket directory above, but a project that ran it still has the file.
plugins/graphify/.density-generation

# The atomic-write temp files. Each writer uses an exclusive name
# (`.NAME.tmp-<pid>-<random>`) so concurrent writers cannot publish each
# other's half-written file; they exist only for the duration of the write.
plugins/graphify/.*.tmp-*

# The rebuild lock is a transient, machine-local mutex. It exists only while a
# rebuild is running, so it must never be shared between machines.
plugins/graphify/.density-rebuild.lock
# A lock that was moved aside while it was being reclaimed or released. The
# move is atomic and the file is removed right after it was inspected, but a
# crash in that window can leave one behind: it is local runtime state.
plugins/graphify/.density-rebuild.lock.move-*

# The pending-update queue. It records an incremental update that had to
# wait for the rebuild lock; it is local runtime state.
plugins/graphify/.density-pending/

# A legacy reminder that was CLAIMED by the rebuild: the single file is
# moved aside with an atomic rename before it is answered, and a crash in
# the window between the move and the cleanup can leave the claimed file
# behind. It is local runtime state of the plugin, and the reminder it
# holds is never lost: the claim is replayed into the record model.
plugins/graphify/.density-claimed/

# The commit marker of a publication: it names the digests of the files
# that were published, so a reader can tell whether the graph and the
# manifest next to it belong together. It is runtime state of the plugin.
plugins/graphify/.density-commit.json

# Graphify detached full-rebuild logs. The runtime-artifact convention puts
# every artifact under .agent/, never in the temp directory; the logs are
# local-only and must not enter the source tree.
logs/graphify/

# The density guard state is isolated per worktree: two worktrees of one
# repository must not share a lock, a baseline or a pending request.
plugins/graphify/.density-worktrees/

# Scheduled-run evidence: `.agent/schedules/*.json` definitions are tracked
# config, but each tick writes a run record containing batch ids and reply
# excerpts. Those records are local runtime state and must not enter the
# source tree.
runtime/schedules/
